            0.062,
            0.224,
            0.282,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sipra Guha Mukherjee",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in collaboration with conab Witnl"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.061,
            0.249,
            0.315,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "S. C. Maheshwari",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " made a breakthrough discovery in the area of plant tissue culture. Their research led to the development of a complete plant through anther culture, using an artificial nutrient medium under controlled laboratory conditions. This pioneering work greatly contributed to crop improvement and brought significant progress in modern agriculture."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.044,
            0.545,
            0.107,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.044,
                0.545,
                0.107,
                0.609
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.576,
            0.318,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.088,
            0.604,
            0.33,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In young plants, the outer protective layer is a single-layered epidermis. As plants grow older, some cells below the epidermis of the stem develop the ability to divide, act as lateral meristematic cells and form the cork cambium. The division of cork cambium cells gives rise to cork cells. Cork cells are dead, compactly arranged, and contain a substance which makes them impermeable to water and gases. This forms the bark of the tree."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.088,
            0.89,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ground tissue system:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This forms the main body of a plant between the dermal and conducting tissues. It includes parenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.143,
            0.889,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Vascular tissue system:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This consists of conducting tissues — xylem and phloem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.189,
            0.613,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.377,
            0.217,
            0.87,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Why do you think that a thick cuticle on the outer wall of epidermis is advantageous for a plant living in the desert but disadvantageous for a plant living underwater?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.377,
            0.267,
            0.87,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Once water is absorbed by plant roots, it has to travel against gravity through xylem. How do the ‘dead’ cells of the xylem work together with the living cells of leaves at the top to keep the water moving?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.376,
            0.332,
            0.868,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. What do you think will happen if there were no stomata in the epidermis of the stem or leaves?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.353,
            0.401,
            0.596,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.3 Animal Tissues"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.352,
            0.425,
            0.889,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Like plants, animal cells also group together and specialised in performing different functions. These groups of similar cells form animal tissues. Let us perform a few simple activities —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.497,
            0.601,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Blink your eyes quickly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.517,
            0.621,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Clench and open your fist."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.538,
            0.564,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a deep breath."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.559,
            0.67,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Touch something warm or cold."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.579,
            0.889,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now think, which tissue helps you move? Which tissue enables you to sense heat or cold? Which tissue allows oxygen to enter the blood? Which tissue holds the body together so that the skin does not fall off?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.368,
            0.65,
            0.89,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Many such questions can be asked but the answers lie in the diversity of animal tissues, which are specially adapted to perform different functions. It is interesting to understand how the structure of an animal tissue suits its specific function."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.37,
            0.738,
            0.889,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us explore different kinds of animal tissues. Each tissue performs a specific function."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.37,
            0.782,
            0.807,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.3.1 Epithelial tissues — Structure and functions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.355,
            0.824,
            0.891,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Epithelial tissue forms the outer covering of the body (skin) and also lines the internal organs, such as the mouth, lungs, blood vessels and intestine. It is composed of closely packed cells with very little space between them. This structure prevents the entry of the germs, reduces"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.943,
            0.109,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "34"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
